{"results":[{"url":"https://www.npr.org/corrections/","final_url":"https://www.npr.org/corrections/","title":"NPR Corrections : NPR","description":null,"language":"en","text":"### Corrected on November 4, 2026\n\nThis story previously misquoted Natalie Silver.\n\n### Corrected on November 5, 2026\n\nThis story previously misstated the number of seats Republicans need in the Senate to sustain a governor's veto.\n\n### Corrected on November 4, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this story misstated the number of Republican candidates for the Broome County Legislature who won their races.\n\n### Corrected on November 3, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this story said Roy Cooper left office in 2024. He left office in early 2025.\n\nAll Things Considered\nNovember 3, 2026\n\n### Corrected on November 4, 2026\n\nA previous headline incorrectly said data centers are being welcomed in Western Kentucky. In fact, they are being welcomed in Eastern Kentucky.\n\n### Corrected on November 3, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story incorrectly included Tulsi Gabbard in a list of prominent Indian Americans.\n\n### Corrected on November 2, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story incorrectly said that Tim Chambers was an employee of Innovating for the Public Good. He works for the Dewey Square Group, which conducted research on behalf of IFPG.\n\nMorning Edition\nNovember 2, 2026\n\n### Corrected on November 2, 2026\n\nA previous version of this web story misquoted JD Vance as saying that his first job was at Dhillon Foods. He said Dillman Foods.\n\n### Corrected on November 2, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this story incorrectly stated the partisan makeup of the New Hampshire State Senate. It has been corrected to reflect that Republicans outnumber Democrats 16-8.\n\n### Corrected on November 1, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story incorrectly referred to Marcel Hug and Catherine Debrunner as Swedish. They are from Switzerland.\n\n## In the '70s and '80s, 'SCTV' spoke directly to me\n\n### Corrected on October 31, 2026\n\nAn earlier photo caption incorrectly identified Joe Flaherty as Dave Thomas.\n\nTED Radio Hour\nOctober 30, 2026\n\n## The future of conservation is live-streamed\n\n### Corrected on November 1, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of the headline on this story incorrectly said the topic was “conversation.” It’s conservation.\n\n### Corrected on November 2, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story incorrectly said the Minnesota U.S. Senate seat up for election in 2026 is open because Sen. Amy Klobuchar is running for governor. It is open because Sen. Tina Smith is retiring.\n\nMorning Edition\nOctober 30, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 30, 2026\n\nThis story incorrectly refers to the outlet where reporter Troy Brynelson works as Oregon Public Radio. It is Oregon Public Broadcasting.\n\n### Corrected on October 29, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story incorrectly spelled Glenn Coffee's name.\n\nAll Things Considered\nOctober 29, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 29, 2026\n\nThis story has been updated to remove a photo of Patrick Stewart in character as Jean-Luc Picard that had been reversed by the agency that provided it.\n\nMorning Edition\nOctober 29, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 29, 2026\n\nA previous headline incorrectly said that refugees in 11 states are losing Medicaid coverage. In fact, most refugees nationwide are losing it, because of a change in federal law. States can continue to provide Medicaid coverage to refugees who are children or who are pregnant, but 11 of them are not doing so.\n\nMorning Edition\nOctober 28, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 29, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story misattributed the quote, \"They have our information, so it's only fair that we have theirs,\" to the DDoS. It was written by the Department of Peace.\n\n### Corrected on October 28, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story incorrectly said that the Senate special committee on aging hearing where Meghan Maher spoke was held in April. It took place in May.\n\n### Corrected on October 28, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this story referred incorrectly to the shortened name of FWD.us as FWRD. It is FWD.\n\nMorning Edition\nOctober 27, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 27, 2026\n\nA previous version of a chart in this story incorrectly said that one of the possible responses to a question was \"government is almost always wasteful and efficient.\" The possible response was \"government is almost always wasteful and inefficient.\"\n\n### Corrected on October 26, 2026\n\nA previous version of this article wrongly stated that 4,580 measles cases had been reported since Oct. 22 across the U.S. That is in fact the number reported until Oct. 22 for 2026.\n\nAll Things Considered\nOctober 24, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 24, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story mistakenly stated Sarah Harris’ age as 37 when she gave birth to her first child. She was 36.\n\nMorning Edition\nOctober 23, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 28, 2026\n\nThe audio version of this story incorrectly says that if the incumbent, Jon Husted, wins in November, he will serve his first full term in the U.S Senate. Husted was appointed to the Senate seat vacated by JD Vance last year, and whoever wins this year's election will serve out the remainder of Vance's term, through 2028.\n\n### Corrected on October 23, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story incorrectly identified the group that makes retention recommendations. It is the Judicial Performance Review Committee, an independent body established under Missouri's constitution.\n\n### Corrected on October 27, 2026\n\nThis story was updated to more accurately summarize the KBA's surveys.\n\n### Corrected on October 22, 2026\n\nThis report has been updated to reflect the correct date of Election Day, which is Nov. 3.\n\nMorning Edition\nOctober 21, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 21, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this web story incorrectly stated that the U.S. and Russia are the top per capita emitters of greenhouse gases in the G20. In fact, Saudi Arabia has higher per capita emissions and is a member of the G20. The U.S. and Russia last year had the highest per capita emissions among the world’s largest emitters of greenhouse gases.\n\n### Corrected on October 21, 2026\n\nThis article has been corrected to include that Kelly Forbes is from Broken Arrow and not Broken Bow.\n\n### Corrected on October 20, 2026\n\nThis article has been updated to remove an incorrect descriptor of Rep. Matt Birong's role in the Vermont House of Representatives.\n\nAll Things Considered\nOctober 19, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 26, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this web story misquoted Republican Aaron Flint as saying he was running for office because of damage done to \"this party\" by Democrats. Flint said he was running because of what he described as damage done to \"this country\" by Democrats.\n\nAll Things Considered\nOctober 18, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 20, 2026\n\nA previous photo credit misspelled Antony Nagelmann's first name as Anthony.\n\n### Corrected on October 17, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story incorrectly referred to the group Palestine Action as Palestinian Action.\n\n### Corrected on October 16, 2026\n\nThis story has been updated to correct that greater oversight of data centers is part of the Democratic Party platform.\n\nAll Things Considered\nOctober 15, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 16, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this story incorrectly said that 247 Palestinian journalists have been killed by Israel's military in Gaza. In fact, that’s the number of journalists and people who support their work who have been killed across the Middle East since Oct. 7, 2023, according to the Committee to Protect Journalists. Of those, 189 were killed in Gaza.\n\n### Corrected on November 3, 2026\n\nPrevious audio and web versions of this story mischaracterized chest rubs made with eucalyptus as a homeopathic remedy. They are not. The audio version also incorrectly said honey is a homeopathic remedy.\n\nFresh Air\nOctober 14, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 15, 2026\n\nThe audio of this story incorrectly states that the Coast Guard is part of the Commerce Department. In fact, the Coast Guard is part of the Department of Homeland Security (during peacetime). Additionally, in the audio of this story, as in a previous web version, we incorrectly refer to the Coast Guard Cutter Healy as the USS Healy. The correct designation is the USCGC Healy.\n\nAll Things Considered\nOctober 14, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 14, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this story said that inflation was higher in 2002, following Russia's invasion of Ukraine. The invasion and the resulting spike in prices were in 2022.\n\nAll Things Considered\nOctober 14, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 14, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this story misspelled Karime Rodriguez's first name as Carime.\n\nMorning Edition\nOctober 12, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 12, 2026\n\nA previous headline incorrectly referred to Texas’ 15th congressional district as the 5th.\n\nWeekend Edition Sunday\nOctober 11, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 13, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this story incorrectly said the protests in India have been going on for months. In fact, they’ve been going on for weeks.\n\nWeekend Edition Sunday\nOctober 11, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 21, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story misspelled Aarhus University as Aarhaus, Mathias Clasen as Matthias Clausen and Marc Andersen as Marc Anderssen in one instance.\n\nWeekend Edition Sunday\nOctober 11, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 12, 2026\n\nThe audio of this story and a previous version of the web version mischaracterize Evan Smith's former employer as an RV dealership in Colorado. Smith worked for Trader Interactive, an online platform for trading RVs and other vehicles around the country.\n\n### Corrected on October 14, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story incorrectly said that Tadaryl Shipp was sentenced to life without parole. In fact, he was sentenced to life in prison with the possibility of parole.\n\nAll Things Considered\nOctober 9, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 16, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this story incorrectly referred to Evan Milligan as an attorney. He graduated from law school, but does not practice law.\n\nFresh Air\nOctober 9, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 13, 2026\n\nThe audio version of this story incorrectly says that John Prine’s song\"Sam Stone” is about a Korean War veteran. In fact, it is about a veteran of the Vietnam War. Also, a previous web introduction to this story misstated Prine's last name as Pine.\n\n### Corrected on October 14, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story incorrectly said that Tadaryl Shipp was sentenced to life without parole. In fact, he was sentenced to life in prison with the possibility of parole.\n\n### Corrected on October 8, 2026\n\nIn a previous version of this story, The Associated Press erroneously reported that Kenso Soai is 95 years old. In fact, Henri B. Kagan is 95, and Soai is 76.\n\nMorning Edition\nOctober 7, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 8, 2026\n\nA previously version of this story misspelled one instance of Jessica Sims' last name as Simms.\n\nMorning Edition\nOctober 7, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 13, 2026\n\nIn the audio of this story, as in a previous web version, we incorrectly say that BTS fans came up with the term “namjooning.” In fact, Kim Nam-joon used the word with its current meaning on Weverse in 2019 and it caught on from there.\n\nWeekend Edition Sunday\nOctober 7, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 7, 2026\n\nAn earlier caption for a photo of Veronica Spears and Marc Schindler incorrectly said the photo was taken at the gravesite in Cheltenham, Md. In fact, it was taken in Fort Washington, Md.\n\n### Corrected on October 6, 2026\n\nA previous photo caption incorrectly said Jim Bakker's Heritage USA theme park was in Columbia, South Carolina. It was in Fort Mill, South Carolina.\n\nFresh Air\nOctober 5, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 9, 2026\n\nThe audio version of this story incorrectly states that the 1981 *East of Eden* miniseries featured Sam Bottoms as Cal. In fact, the actor playing Cal was Timothy Bottoms.\n\nAll Things Considered\nOctober 4, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 4, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story misspelled Holly Andersen's last name as Anderson.\n\nWeekend Edition Saturday\nOctober 3, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 28, 2026\n\nThis story incorrectly says that the Milwaukee Brewers won 102 games during the 2026 regular season and have never been to the World Series. In fact, the Brewers won 103 regular-season games and lost the 1982 World Series to the St. Louis Cardinals 4 games to 3.\n\nWeekend Edition Saturday\nOctober 3, 2026\n\n## Theaster Gates bets on fine china\n\n### Corrected on October 3, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story said Theaster Gates is a professor at the University of Chicago. In fact, he is no longer affiliated with the university.\n\nWeekend Edition Saturday\nOctober 3, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 9, 2026\n\nIn the audio of this story, as in a previous web version, we misstate Tyrome Launderville's last name as Laundersville\n\n### Corrected on October 5, 2026\n\n***Editor's note****: This post has been updated to reflect that in La Mesa, Santee and Vista, if the measures pass, the resulting property taxes would largely extend existing tax rates, not add to them.*\n\n### Corrected on October 2, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this story misspelled Karianne Jones' first name as Kariane.\n\n### Corrected on October 4, 2026\n\nAn earlier photo caption incorrectly referred to the panda Fu Shuang as male. Fu Shuang is female.**Previously posted Oct. 2, 2026:** A previous version of the quiz incorrectly misidentified Case Western Reserve University as \"Case Western.\"\n\nAll Things Considered\nOctober 2, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 2, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this story misstated how frequently Americans bet compared to reading a book. Americans are more likely to have placed a bet in the last year than have read a book in the same time period.\n\n### Corrected on October 3, 2026\n\nThis story was updated to correct District 7's coverage area. The district now covers part of Cuyahoga, Medina and Wayne counties, and all of Ashland County. Due to redistricting, Holmes County is no longer included.\n\nAll Things Considered\nOctober 1, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 2, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story incorrectly claimed that Google's satellite would be in sun-synchronous orbit and not require batteries. While that is the plan for future satellites, this prototype is in a standard orbit and uses batteries.\n\n### Corrected on October 1, 2026\n\nThis story was updated to correct the spelling of Kevin Schilling's name.\n\nMorning Edition\nOctober 1, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 6, 2026\n\nIn this report, the guest misstates the first name of Todd Beamer, a passenger on United Airlines Flight 93 on 9/11, as Scott.\n\nAll Things Considered\nOctober 1, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 1, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story incorrectly said Aroostook County, Maine, is the northernmost in the contiguous U.S. Lake of the Woods County, Minnesota, holds that distinction.\n\nAll Things Considered\nSeptember 30, 2026\n\n### Corrected on September 30, 2026\n\n*This article was revised to correct the initial Zyprexa dosage given to Marjorie Tingley.*\n\nMorning Edition\nSeptember 29, 2026\n\n### Corrected on September 30, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story incorrectly said the Navy had not responded to NPR by deadline.\n\n## Should you test out ‘The Love Hypothesis’?\n\n### Corrected on September 28, 2026\n\nA previous version of this episode's web description misspelled Anh, the first name of Olive's best friend in *The Love Hypothesis*, as Ahn.\n\nAll Things Considered\nSeptember 25, 2026\n\n### Corrected on October 5, 2026\n\nA previous web introduction to this report misspelled Dolly Parton's last name as Party.\n\nAll Things Considered\nSeptember 25, 2026\n\n### Corrected on September 25, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story incorrectly said that the U.S. government, in partnership with a company in Venezuela, would get a majority stake in 17 Venezuelan oil fields. In fact, the U.S. government would get a share of the output from the Venezuelan oil fields.\n\nMorning Edition\nSeptember 24, 2026\n\n### Corrected on September 24, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this story incorrectly identified Micah Stopperich as male. In fact, she's female.\n\nAll Things Considered\nSeptember 23, 2026\n\n### Corrected on September 24, 2026\n\nIn an earlier version of the host’s introduction in the audio, we incorrectly said that Sliman Mansour died last week. He died in August.\n\nMorning Edition\nSeptember 23, 2026\n\n## ICE is looking into 'mobile detention facilities'\n\n### Corrected on September 24, 2026\n\n*This story has been updated to clarify that Todd Lyons was acting ICE director when the expansion in immigrant detention centers began last year. He no longer serves in that capacity.*\n\nAll Things Considered\nSeptember 21, 2026\n\n### Corrected on September 21, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this story incorrectly said that Paramount's acquisition of Warner Bros. would include the Star Wars franchise. In fact, the Star Wars franchise is owned by Disney.\n\n### Corrected on September 20, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story incorrectly said that Rafael Garces Perez is 29. He is 28.\n\nWeekend Edition Sunday\nSeptember 20, 2026\n\n## Sunday Puzzle: Roadway anagrams\n\n### Corrected on September 24, 2026\n\n*This puzzle* *incorrectly states that NEARSIGHTED + L is an anagram of RIGHT LANE ENDS. In fact, the anagram needs another N to be correct.*\n\n### Corrected on September 19, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this story incorrectly said the Federal Reserve under Jerome Powell started cutting rates after inflation started to climb. In fact, the Fed raised rates.\n\nAll Things Considered\nSeptember 18, 2026\n\n### Corrected on September 23, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this story incorrectly said that rootstock is grafted onto trees. In fact, a cutting from a tree is grafted on the rootstock.\n\nAll Things Considered\nSeptember 18, 2026\n\n### Corrected on September 21, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this story incorrectly said that Google’s lobbyists registered in support of a chatbot bill in Colorado. They registered in support of amendments to the chatbot bill.\n\n## How two swing states changed American voting\n\n### Corrected on September 18, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this episode incorrectly said that Michigan voters could send the first Arab American to the Senate. South Dakotan James Abourezk became the first Arab American U.S. senator in 1973.\n\nMorning Edition\nSeptember 17, 2026\n\n### Corrected on September 17, 2026\n\nPrevious audio and web versions of this story misidentified Barbara Duffield, executive director of SchoolHouse Connection, as Pat Holterman-Hommes, president of the St. Louis-based Youth in Need. Also, the digital version incorrectly said public comment on the proposed changes to Head Start was open until Oct. 7. It is open until Oct. 6.\n\nAll Things Considered\nSeptember 14, 2026\n\n### Corrected on September 17, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story incorrectly said that the 140 people who recently tested positive for HIV in Karachi were children. In fact, those cases were mostly children but included some adults. Also, the story incorrectly said that those cases had been linked to everyday medical care. Not all cases have been conclusively linked to such care.\n\nAll Things Considered\nSeptember 14, 2026\n\n### Corrected on September 14, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this story incorrectly said Bob Mackie won 10 Emmys. He won nine, plus a Tony Award.\n\nWeekend Edition Sunday\nSeptember 13, 2026\n\n### Corrected on September 13, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story incorrectly referred to Frank Ahrens as the head of analytics at the BRG Group. He works for BGR Group.\n\n### Corrected on September 14, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this story included an unrelated photo, which has been replaced.\n\n### Corrected on September 11, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this story incorrectly said Paul Keating attended 9/11 events in New York on Friday. He did not.\n\nAll Things Considered\nSeptember 10, 2026\n\n### Corrected on September 10, 2026\n\nA previous version of this web story mistakenly said that Republican Ron DeSantis is the governor of Texas. In fact, he is the governor of Florida.\n\n## How baseball got its beat\n\n### Corrected on September 10, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this episode incorrectly said that the Brooklyn Dodgers moved to Los Angeles in 1952. The team moved ahead of the 1958 season.\n\n### Corrected on September 11, 2026\n\nIn a previous version of this story, The Associated Press erroneously reported that a UPS plane crash at the Louisville airport took place in October 2025. It happened in November of that year.\n\n### Corrected on September 11, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story referred to the weapon that a National Guard member allegedly pointed at another as a service revolver. The arrest affidavit does not specify the type of firearm.\n\nAll Things Considered\nSeptember 8, 2026\n\n### Corrected on September 8, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story misspelled the *Star Trek* character Nurse Chapel's last name as Chappell. It also incorrectly said that the title sequence for the original television series included the line \"to boldly go where no one has gone before.\" In fact, the line was \"to boldly go where no man has gone before.\"\n\nAll Things Considered\nSeptember 7, 2026\n\n### Corrected on September 8, 2026\n\nAn earlier caption incorrectly identified the people in the photo on Tara Gentner's phone as Gentner and her mother, Sheri Petty. In fact, the photo shows Petty and Gentner’s daughter, Hillary. Also, a previous version of this web story incorrectly said that Tara Gentner's daughter was living in Petty’s house at the time of Petty's death. Only Petty and Gentner were living there.\n\n### Corrected on September 8, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story incorrectly said that an Engineered Material Arresting System is not required if a runway is longer than 1,000 feet. In fact, one is not required if there is a 1,000-foot runway safety area.\n\n### Corrected on September 8, 2026\n\nA previous version of the quiz on this page sometimes incorrectly indicated that a user was wrong when they had correctly answered that Douglas Adams was the famous procrastinator.\n\nAll Things Considered\nSeptember 4, 2026\n\n### Corrected on September 14, 2026\n\nIn this interview, the guest, while noting that much of the World Trade Center steel was exported for recycling, incorrectly says that steel recycling no longer happens in the U.S. In fact, more than 50 million metric tons of steel scrap are recycled in the U.S. each year, according to the American Iron and Steel Institute.\n\n### Corrected on September 5, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story misspelled Colombia as Columbia.\n\n### Corrected on September 4, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story misspelled the Tdap vaccine as Tdao.\n\n### Corrected on September 4, 2026\n\nA previous version of this story incorrectly said that stocks opened higher Friday morning after new inflation data was released. In fact, stocks opened mixed in response to a stronger-than-expected jobs report, and the referenced inflation data was released last month. The story also incorrectly said that the Dow Jones Industrial Average rose about 100 points in early trading. It actually opened down.\n\nMorning Edition\nSeptember 3, 2026\n\n### Corrected on September 4, 2026\n\nAn earlier version of this article incorrectly said Bartiromo was last on air on Thursday. She was last on Fox in August. Her show bore her name until Thursday. The earlier version also said incorrectly that a Fox Business executive told network shows that President Trump’s speech would not be carried live. In fact, the executive told the shows not to cover the speech the day after it was delivered.","author":null,"published_date":"November 3, 2026","latency_ms":1566.595825002878,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}